    2  A   ablation_onset           2.718    85.65   1.592e+05  stylized
          Net heating flux first exceeds radiative loss at T_ablate = 2300 K, so ablation switches on
          (a kink in the slope). Real onset is gradual over a few km. BELOW this point the model's
          light is drag work only (no ablated material): treat light above ~86 km as low-confidence.
          Loosely like the end of Phase 1 / start of Phase 2 in [R22], but the model has no spin,
          volatile (Na/K) emission or melt physics.
    3  Y   first_yield              7.284    62.31   1.409e+07  physical
          Loading rho v^2 reaches S0 = 0.09 MPa. The body starts to flatten (pancake): area, drag and
          heating all grow, so the light rises faster. Analogue of the onset of fragmentation (Phase 5
          / H_f in [R22]).
    4  F   flare                    7.331    62.08   3.706e+07  stylized
          Light rises x5.6 in 0.17 s (steeper than 3x the air-density tracking rate). Associated with:
          yield gen 0 at 62.3 km (99% of m0); split gen 0 at 61.9 km (99% of m0). Dust groups supply
          46% of the rise and 38% of the light at the end of the flare. A real fragmentation flare
          (Phase 5 in [R22]); its sharpness and timing here come from the model's deterministic
          cascade.
    5  B   first_breakup            7.362    61.92   6.194e+07  stylized
          Spread factor reached f_split = 2: the body breaks into 4 children plus 20 % of its mass as
          dust. The dust has a large area-to-mass ratio, decelerates and ablates within a fraction of
          a second, which makes a sharp flare. The instantaneous release is a model simplification.
          Light grew x4.4 between first yield and this breakup (pancake spreading).
    6  P   peak                    10.287    48.09   1.631e+08  physical
          Maximum light. It sits ~+3.8 km from the peak of energy deposition (44.2 km) because the
          luminous efficiency tau(v) falls as the fragments slow (mass-weighted speed here 17.8 km/s).
    7  -   drag_overtakes_ablation  12.267    40.16   8.945e+07  stylized
          Drag work now exceeds the kinetic energy carried off by ablated mass. The ratio
          ablation/drag is about Lambda v^2/(2 Q Cd), so this happens when the speed falls below ~14
          km/s (from the model's heating law).
    8  D   rolloff                 12.732    38.62   5.994e+07  physical
          Light has fallen to 1/e of the peak 2.45 s after it. Cause: the kinetic energy is being used
          up (speed 65% of v0, 32% of the mass still in flight) and tau(v) drops with speed. Analogue
